    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ef90b9cc_2cb4f139",
        "filename": "chip/g/jitter.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 90,
      "author": {
        "id": 1126264
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-07-30T20:40:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So the code was meant to calculate the 1250ppm cutoff for the sof trim buckets.  The problem is, the targetCnt*(1000000-1250) overflowed the 32b values and caused the buckets to be completely wrong.  \n\nI\u0027m sure there is a much smarter way to deal with this, but I just did the easy thing and made it a 64b int.",
      "parentUuid": "ef90b9cc_cc70eda4",
      "revId": "375da28a8b81b54e64cbe84209b8ffba7f6e746c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ef90b9cc_aca1e1ee",
        "filename": "chip/g/jitter.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 90,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002815
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-07-30T21:07:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You could start by taking a factor 10 out of both sides across the division. What\u0027s the max for targetCnt?\n\nAlso, that expression occurs twice here, refactor into a routine which then can carry some documentation about whichever optimization is made to make its computation avoid 64 bit arith.\n\nComputers aren\u0027t very good doing stuff in base 10. Are these values that significant or can approximate power of 2 values do the trick?",
      "parentUuid": "ef90b9cc_2cb4f139",
      "revId": "375da28a8b81b54e64cbe84209b8ffba7f6e746c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ef90b9cc_6cec3926",
        "filename": "chip/g/jitter.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 90,
      "author": {
        "id": 1126264
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-07-30T21:18:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "that\u0027s what I did at first, but then I realized that would place a maximum constraint on targetCnt, and I thought maybe making it that specialized wasn\u0027t a good idea.  Target Count will end up directly correlating to how fast the system clock is. So at 24MHz, it is 24000, at 100Mhz, it will be 100k, so on and so forth.   I did not actually work through the math to see how much a divide by 10 factor would cover, just said screw it and did 64bits.  \n\nI did not think about power of 2s, let me take a look.  The 1250ppm requirement is apparently important based on USB spec, although in practice it will vary on HOST/DEVICE combination.\n\nWhat\u0027s the general feeling here though? Should this be made generic enough to cover arbitrary frequencies (within reason?)",
      "parentUuid": "ef90b9cc_aca1e1ee",
      "revId": "375da28a8b81b54e64cbe84209b8ffba7f6e746c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
